From 40351f10d9be479e9dc1cf02c943054e6940c161 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 20:13:12 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Stiffness matrices computed by kernels, geometry without the
 nodes of the mesh
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- `Mesh::GetGeometricFactors` gives nodes to a mesh which has none
  (`Mesh::EnsureNodes`), and so does `QuadratureInterpolator::
  PhysDerivatives`: all the element transformations then use the nodes,
  and the computations element by element which remain (residual, tangent
  operator, prediction) were about 50% slower (poutre case of the
  prototype). The geometric data of the kernels (`batch::ElementsGeometry`,
  in its own module) are now computed from a copy of the vertices for a
  mesh without nodes, once for each integration rule, and the gradients
  use the derivatives with respect to the reference coordinates, mapped by
  the inverse of the jacobians in the kernel.
- Stiffness matrices of the elements, `Σ Bᵀ K B w det J` (one thread per
  pair of nodes of an element, the operator `B` of the generated behaviour
  integrators, tangent operator blocks in the global frame), stored
  element by element as `mfem::DenseMatrix` (`batch::addStiffnessMatrices`,
  `AbstractBehaviourIntegrator::addStiffnessMatricesByKernels`,
  `MultiMaterialNonLinearIntegrator::computeStiffnessMatricesByKernels`).
- New parameter `TangentOperatorComputation` (`ElementByElement` or
  `Kernels`, default given by `MFEM_MGIS_TANGENT_OPERATOR_COMPUTATION`):
  `GetGradient` computes the stiffness matrices by kernels, which
  `AssembleElementGrad` gives to the assembly of MFEM; refused with PETSc.
- Functions shared by the kernels in `src/Batch/KernelsUtilities.hxx`.

Tests: GradientsKernelsTest also compares the stiffness matrices to the
ones computed element by element (differences of the order of 1e-16, on
the host, on the debug device and on the GPU); BatchIntegrationTest
compares complete computations with the stiffness matrices computed by
kernels. 142/142; with all the kernels enabled, only the expected
refusals fail (heat transfer, micromorphic damage).
